// Field Guide cards first taught in the K camp (Hillary Step: The Auditor).
// Every project, company and service named here is invented (and uses the reserved
// .example domain). npm and git commands and outputs follow real npm 10 / git 2.4x.
// Licence facts are general education, not legal advice.

export const GUIDE_CAMPK = {
  // ─────────────── The method ───────────────
  'audit-method': {
    term: 'Audit method', short: 'A repeatable way to judge anything: scope, questions, evidence, verdict.',
    what: "An **audit** is a careful, evidence-based judgement of a project, product or tool. It answers one practical question, like \"should our team use this library?\" or \"is this app safe to launch?\", and it ends with a clear **verdict** plus a list of what must change.\n\nAn audit is not an opinion. Anyone should be able to follow your notes, re-check every finding, and reach the same conclusion.",
    how: "Every audit, big or small, runs the same four phases: scope, questions, evidence, verdict.\n\n**1. Scope.** Write down, before you start:\n• **the question** you are answering (\"Is TrailPost safe to recommend to our club?\")\n• **what is in** (the code, the README, the dependencies, the pricing page) and **what is out** (their live servers, which you have no permission to test)\n• **a time box** (\"2 hours\"), so the audit doesn't grow forever\n• **the rules**: read-only first. You never run code you haven't read, and you never change the thing you're auditing.\n\n**2. Questions.** Work through a checklist of areas. This camp gives you one trail per area:\n• **Claims**: what does it promise, and is there proof?\n• **Health**: is anyone maintaining it?\n• **Dependencies**: what other code comes with it?\n• **Architecture and threats**: what are the parts, what's valuable, how could someone get in?\n• **Secrets**: are passwords or keys sitting in the files?\n• **Cost**: what will it really cost, now and at 10× the users?\n• **Privacy**: what personal data goes where, for how long?\n• **Licence**: are you even allowed to use it the way you plan to?\n• **Performance**: do the benchmarks hold up?\n\n**3. Evidence.** For each answer, collect something anyone can re-check: a file and line number, a command and its output, an exact quote, a measurement. Keep a running notes file **outside** the project folder. Label everything as one of:\n• **observed** (you saw it yourself)\n• **claimed** (they say so)\n• **assumed** (you're guessing, and you say so)\n\n**4. Verdict.** Give every finding a **severity** (how bad if it goes wrong × how likely it is), then make one call:\n• **ADOPT**: no serious findings\n• **ADOPT WITH FIXES**: usable once named problems are fixed\n• **DO NOT ADOPT**: a serious problem with no quick fix\n\nThe last phase is writing it up: see the **Audit report** card.",
    usage: [
      { code: 'QUESTION: Should our climbing club use TrailPost to share trip plans?\nIN SCOPE: the public repo, README, package.json, pricing page\nOUT OF SCOPE: their live servers (no permission)\nTIME BOX: 2 hours, read-only', note: 'A scope statement. Four lines, written before you open a single file.' },
      { code: '$ echo "HIGH: .env with a database password is in the repo (observed)" >> ~/audit-notes.txt\n$ cat ~/audit-notes.txt', out: 'HIGH: .env with a database password is in the repo (observed)', note: 'Evidence goes into a notes file in your home folder, never inside the project you are auditing.' },
      { code: 'Severity = impact × likelihood\n  HIGH    a stranger could read every user\'s trips today\n  MEDIUM  a real risk, but it needs unusual conditions\n  LOW     worth fixing, little harm if it happens', note: 'A simple severity scale. Agree it before you start rating.' },
      { code: 'VERDICT: DO NOT ADOPT\nReason: 2 high-severity findings (leaked database password,\ninstall script that uploads your .env). Re-audit after both are fixed.', note: 'A verdict is one clear call plus the reason. Never "it depends" with nothing after it.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Choosing between two libraries, two hosting services or two apps.', 'Answering a teammate who says "just install this, it\'s great".', 'Checking a product before your team pays for it.', 'Auditing your **own** app before you launch it, which is exactly what the Summit camp asks you to do.', 'Reviewing an open-source project before you contribute to it or depend on it.'],
    when: ['Before you install, depend on, pay for, ship or recommend anything.', 'When a claim sounds too good, or a decision would be expensive to undo.', 'Before a launch, and again whenever the project changes a lot.'],
    whenNot: ['Never test systems you don\'t own or have written permission to test. Reading a public repo is fine; probing someone else\'s live servers can be illegal.', 'Don\'t run untrusted code "to see what it does" during an audit. If you truly must run it, use a throwaway machine with no secrets on it.', 'Don\'t let an audit become endless. When the time box runs out, report what you found and list what you didn\'t check.', 'For high-stakes cases (medical, money, legal contracts), a learner\'s audit is a first pass, not a replacement for a professional one.'],
    mistakes: [
      { prose: 'Starting to poke around with no written question or scope.', why: 'You end up with a pile of random observations and no answer. Write the question first; it tells you what matters.' },
      { prose: 'Running `npm install` on the project to "see what happens".', why: 'Install scripts run instantly with your permissions. Read package.json and the scripts first. That *is* the audit.' },
      { prose: 'Writing findings like "the code seems bad" with no file, line or output.', why: 'Nobody can check or fix a feeling. Every finding needs evidence someone else can re-check.' },
      { prose: 'Only checking the code, and forgetting cost, privacy and licence.', why: 'A project can have perfect code and still be unaffordable, illegal to use your way, or sending your users\' data to strangers.' },
    ],
    tips: ['Keep your notes file in your home folder, like `~/audit-notes.txt`, so you never accidentally change or commit the project you\'re auditing.', 'Say what you did **not** check. "Didn\'t test the live site (out of scope)" is honest and useful.', 'Time box it: a 2-hour audit that finishes beats a perfect one that never does.', 'Assume nothing: check every claim yourself before you rely on it.'],
    related: ['claims-evidence', 'audit-report', 'threat-model', 'red-flags'],
    os: {
      phone: 'You can do the reading half of an audit on a phone: open the repo in a browser, read the README, package.json, the commits page and the issues. Running commands like `npm audit` needs a computer.',
    },
  },

  'claims-evidence': {
    term: 'Claims vs evidence', short: 'A claim is what they say. Evidence is what you can check. Judge only on evidence.',
    what: "A **claim** is any statement a project makes about itself: \"100% private\", \"10× faster\", \"actively maintained\", \"bank-grade security\". **Evidence** is something you can check yourself that supports or contradicts it.\n\nThe rule of every audit: **the burden of proof is on the one making the claim.** You don't have to prove \"10× faster\" is false. They have to show it's true. Until they do, the claim is **unverified**.",
    how: "**1. Copy each claim exactly**, word for word, with where you found it. No paraphrasing: the exact wording matters (\"up to 10× faster\" promises almost nothing).\n\n**2. Make it testable.** Rewrite vague words as something you could measure:\n• \"fast\" → \"loads the trip list in under 300 ms (median) on a normal phone\"\n• \"private\" → \"no personal data is sent to any other company\"\n• \"actively maintained\" → \"commits and releases in the last few months, and issues get answered\"\n\n**3. Look for evidence, strongest last.** The evidence ladder:\n• weakest: marketing copy, testimonials, \"trusted by thousands\"\n• popularity: stars, downloads (useful, but stars can be bought)\n• documentation: says how it works, but it's still their words\n• a demo you control: you try it yourself\n• the code itself: you read what it really does\n• independent reproduction: someone with no stake got the same result\n• strongest: **your own measurement**, with your data\n\n**4. Mark each claim:**\n• **SUPPORTED**: evidence backs it\n• **CONTRADICTED**: evidence shows the opposite (the code sends GPS to an ad company, the README says \"private\")\n• **UNVERIFIED**: no evidence either way. Not a lie, just not proven.\n\n**Weasel words** are phrases that sound strong but promise little: \"up to\", \"bank-grade\", \"military-grade encryption\", \"unlimited*\", \"blazing fast\", \"enterprise-ready\", \"AI-powered\", \"100%\". None of them is automatically false; all of them need evidence.",
    usage: [
      { code: 'CLAIM (README)                         EVIDENCE                              MARK\n"100% private: data never leaves      src/analytics.js sends email + GPS    CONTRADICTED\n your device"                           to collect.adwhale.example\n"Actively maintained"                   CHANGELOG last entry 2023-04-02       CONTRADICTED\n"10x faster than other trip apps"      BENCHMARKS.md: only the best run      UNVERIFIED\n"Free forever"                          no pricing page or terms found        UNVERIFIED', note: 'A claims table. Each row: the exact claim, the evidence you found, and your mark.' },
      { code: '"Bank-grade security"', note: 'Ask: which bank, which standard, checked by whom? Restated testably: "passwords are hashed, traffic uses HTTPS, and an independent audit report is published". Then go look for each part.' },
      { code: '"Up to 10x faster"', note: '"Up to" means "at least once, in the best case we found". A single lucky run satisfies it. Ask for the median.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Reading a README, landing page, pitch deck or job ad.', 'Checking a library\'s performance or security promises before you depend on it.', 'Checking your own README before you publish, so you only claim what you can show.'],
    when: ['Any time a decision rests on what a project says about itself.', 'Especially when the claim is dramatic, numeric, or comes from someone selling something.'],
    whenNot: ['Don\'t call an unverified claim a lie. In a public report, "unverified" is accurate and fair; "they lied" is an accusation you can\'t prove.', 'Don\'t demand lab-grade proof for small, low-risk choices. Match the effort to the stakes.'],
    mistakes: [
      { prose: 'Treating "no complaints in the issues" as proof that it\'s secure.', why: 'Absence of evidence is not evidence. Maybe nobody looked, or the issues are switched off.' },
      { prose: 'Paraphrasing the claim ("they say it\'s fast") instead of quoting it.', why: '"Up to 10× faster in one test" and "10× faster" are very different promises. Quote exactly.' },
      { prose: 'Believing a claim because it\'s repeated everywhere.', why: 'Ten blog posts copying the same press release are one source, not ten.' },
    ],
    tips: ['Quote, then test. Every claim gets an exact quote and a mark.', 'The more specific the claim, the more trustworthy the project: "p95 under 200 ms on this benchmark script" beats "blazing fast".', 'Turn every adjective into a measurement before you judge it.'],
    related: ['audit-method', 'benchmark-skepticism', 'red-flags'],
  },

  // ─────────────── Health and dependencies ───────────────
  'repo-health': {
    term: 'Repo health', short: 'Signs that a project is alive and cared for: recent commits, answered issues, more than one maintainer.',
    what: "**Repo health** is how likely a project is to still work, and still get fixes, a year from now. You judge it from signals anyone can see in the repository: its commit history, releases, issues, contributors, tests and docs.\n\nA healthy repo has recent commits, up-to-date docs, and someone who fixes problems when they appear.",
    how: "Check these signals, and write down what you saw:\n• **Last commit and last release.** When was code last changed? When was a version last published? Compare with what the README claims (\"actively maintained\").\n• **Activity over time.** A few commits every month beats 500 commits in one week two years ago.\n• **Issues.** How many are open vs closed? Do maintainers reply, and how fast? Hundreds of open issues with no replies means nobody's home.\n• **Contributors and the bus factor.** The **bus factor** is how many people would have to disappear before the project stalls. One maintainer = bus factor 1: if they burn out or lose interest, the project stops.\n• **Tests and automatic checks (CI).** Does the project test itself on every change?\n• **Docs, changelog and licence.** Can you tell what changed between versions? Is there a licence at all?\n• **Security policy.** A `SECURITY.md` file tells people how to report problems privately.\n• **Archived flag.** An archived repo is read-only: the owner has officially stopped.\n\n**Context matters.** A tiny, finished library (say, one function that pads text) can go quiet for years and still be fine: there's nothing left to fix. A big app that talks to the internet cannot: browsers, security rules and dependencies keep changing under it.\n\n**Weak signals:** stars and forks. Stars are bookmarks, they can be bought, and they never expire, so a dead project keeps its stars forever.",
    usage: [
      { code: '$ git log -1 --format=%cd --date=short', out: '2023-04-02', note: 'The date of the latest commit (real computers). `-1` = only one commit, `%cd` = committer date.' },
      { code: '$ git shortlog -sn HEAD', out: '   212  Kai Ridge\n     3  Sam Col', note: 'Commits per author, most first. One person wrote almost everything: bus factor 1. (Names invented.)' },
      { code: '$ git log --since="1 year ago" --oneline | wc -l', out: '0', note: 'How many commits in the last year. `wc -l` counts lines. Zero means no work at all for a year.' },
      { code: '$ head -n 4 CHANGELOG.md', out: '# Changelog\n\n## 2.3.0 (2023-04-02)\n- Add trip photos', note: 'Works in the practice terminal too: the newest changelog entry tells you when the last release happened.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Choosing between two libraries that do the same job.', 'Deciding whether a dependency you already use needs replacing.', 'Spotting a contradiction between "actively maintained" and a history that stopped years ago.', 'Judging whether a project is safe to contribute to and build on.'],
    when: ['Before adding any dependency your project will rely on for years.', 'During every audit, because an unmaintained project means unfixed security problems.'],
    whenNot: ['Don\'t treat "no commits lately" as automatically bad for a tiny, finished library with no open bugs. Ask whether there is anything left to fix.', 'Don\'t use star counts as a health score. They measure attention once, not care now.', 'Don\'t judge from one signal. Combine several before you decide.'],
    mistakes: [
      { prose: 'Picking the library with the most stars.', why: 'Stars never expire and can be bought. A project with 20k stars may have had no commits for four years.' },
      { prose: 'Trusting the README\'s "actively maintained" badge.', why: 'Anyone can write that line once and never touch the repo again. The commit history is the evidence.' },
      { prose: 'Ignoring the bus factor for something critical.', why: 'If one volunteer holds every key and stops, security fixes stop too. For important dependencies, prefer projects with several active maintainers.' },
    ],
    tips: ['Read the newest open issues first: they show you today\'s problems and whether anyone answers.', 'Look at closed pull requests from outsiders. Merged ones mean the maintainers welcome help.', 'Write the dates in your notes. "Last commit 2023-04-02" is evidence; "old" is an opinion.'],
    related: ['dependency-audit', 'claims-evidence', 'git-log', 'github'],
    os: {
      linux: '`git log`, `git shortlog` and `wc -l` all work as shown. If git is missing: `sudo apt install git` (Ubuntu/Debian), `sudo dnf install git` (Fedora), `sudo pacman -S git` (Arch).',
      mac: 'Same commands. The first `git` command may offer to install the Command Line Tools: accept, or run `xcode-select --install`. Note that the Mac `wc -l` pads its number with spaces (`       0`), which is normal.',
      windows: 'Run these inside WSL (Ubuntu), where git and `wc` behave exactly as shown. Plain PowerShell has no `wc`.',
      chromeos: 'In the Linux environment: `sudo apt install git`, then the commands work as shown.',
      phone: 'On a phone, open the repo in a browser: the **commits** page shows dates, the **Insights** tab (Pulse, Contributors) shows activity and the bus factor, and the **Issues** tab shows whether anyone answers.',
    },
  },

  'dependency-audit': {
    term: 'Dependency audit', short: 'Checking every package a project pulls in: known holes, freshness, install scripts, look-alike names.',
    what: "A **dependency audit** checks all the outside code a project depends on, not just the code its authors wrote. Most of the code in a typical Node app is dependencies, so most of the risk usually is too.\n\nYou already met the dangers in Lhotse Face: typosquats, hijacked packages and install scripts. This card turns that into a step-by-step check.",
    how: "**1. Inventory.** Read `package.json`: list the dependencies, devDependencies and **scripts**. Check that a lock file (`package-lock.json`) exists, so installs are repeatable.\n\n**2. Install scripts first.** Look for `preinstall`, `install` and `postinstall` in scripts. These run automatically on `npm install`, before you've used anything. (A project's own `prepare` script also runs on a plain `npm install` inside it, so read that one too.) Read the file they run. If it sends data anywhere or downloads more code, that's a high-severity finding.\n\n**3. Names, letter by letter.** Compare each name with the popular package it seems to be: `crossenv` vs `cross-env`, `expresss` vs `express`. One letter off is the typosquatting pattern.\n\n**4. Where it comes from.** Versions like `github:user/repo` or a web address skip the npm registry: the code can change at any time with no version history on npm.\n\n**5. Version ranges.** `\"*\"` or `\"latest\"` means \"whatever is newest when you install\", so tomorrow's install can pull in code nobody has reviewed. Prefer normal ranges like `^1.11.13` plus a lock file.\n\n**6. Known vulnerabilities** (real computers): `npm audit` compares your installed versions with a public database of reported problems and rates each **low, moderate, high or critical**. Read where each one sits:\n• is it in a **devDependency** (only on developer machines) or shipped to users? `npm audit --omit=dev` checks only what ships\n• does your app actually use the broken part?\n\n**7. Freshness:** `npm outdated` shows how far behind each package is.\n\n**8. Trust signals per package:** weekly downloads, last publish date, public repository, number of maintainers (see Repo health).\n\n**Fixing, in order of preference:** `npm audit fix` (updates within your allowed ranges), a manual upgrade, replacing the package, or removing it. Be careful with `npm audit fix --force`: it may jump a MAJOR version and break your app.",
    usage: [
      { code: '$ npm audit', out: '# npm audit report\n\ntrail-parser  <2.4.1\nSeverity: high\nPrototype pollution in trail-parser - https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx\nfix available via `npm audit fix`\nnode_modules/trail-parser\n\n1 high severity vulnerability\n\nTo address all issues, run:\n  npm audit fix', note: 'Real computers. The shape of real npm 10 output; the package and advisory are invented examples.' },
      { code: '$ npm outdated', out: 'Package  Current  Wanted  Latest  Location              Depended by\nexpress   4.18.2  4.21.2   5.1.0  node_modules/express  trailpost', note: '**Current** is installed; **Wanted** is the newest your range (`^4.18.2`) allows; **Latest** is the newest published. 5.x is a MAJOR jump: read the upgrade guide first.' },
      { code: '$ npm audit --omit=dev', note: 'Only audits what ships to users. A high-severity hole in a test tool matters less than one in your web server.' },
      { code: '$ npm view trail-parser time.modified repository.url', out: "time.modified = '2023-03-07T23:22:15.505Z'\nrepository.url = 'git+https://code.example/trail-parser.git'", note: 'Look up a package\'s registry info without installing it: when it was last changed and where its source lives. Each field prints as `name = \'value\'`. (Invented package; your output will differ. A package with no repository field prints no line for it.)' },
      { code: '$ npm install --ignore-scripts', note: 'If you must install to inspect, this skips install scripts. (Real computers; your own code may then need a manual build step.)' },
    ],
    uses: ['Before adding a new package to your project.', 'Before adopting someone else\'s project, which brings its whole dependency tree with it.', 'As a regular check-up: new vulnerabilities are reported in old versions all the time.', 'In automatic checks that fail a build on high-severity problems: `npm audit --audit-level=high`.'],
    when: ['Every audit of a Node project, and every time you add or upgrade a dependency.', 'When `npm install` prints "found N vulnerabilities". Read it; don\'t scroll past.'],
    whenNot: ['Don\'t run `npm install` on an untrusted project to audit it. Read package.json and the install scripts first, without installing.', 'Don\'t treat "found 0 vulnerabilities" as "safe". It only means no *known, reported* problem matches. A brand-new malicious package or typosquat usually isn\'t in the database yet.', 'Don\'t run `npm audit fix --force` blindly: it can install breaking major versions.'],
    mistakes: [
      { prose: 'Auditing only the packages listed in package.json.', why: 'Each of those has dependencies of its own. One request for a web framework can bring 60+ packages. `npm audit` checks the whole tree; `npm ls --all` lists it.' },
      { prose: 'Panicking at "3 high severity vulnerabilities" without reading them.', why: 'Check where each one is (a dev tool vs your server) and whether you use the broken feature. Severity is the starting point, not the verdict.' },
      { wrong: '"trail-utils": "*"', result: '(every fresh install takes whatever version is newest that day)', why: 'Nobody reviewed tomorrow\'s version. Use a normal range like `^1.4.0` and commit the lock file.' },
    ],
    tips: ['Read install scripts before anything else. They are the one part of a package that runs without you calling it.', '`npm explain <package>` (or `npm why`) shows which of your dependencies dragged a package in.', 'Fewer dependencies = a shorter audit, forever.'],
    related: ['repo-health', 'dependency', 'npm', 'semver', 'package-json'],
    os: {
      linux: 'npm commands are identical on every system. If `npm` is missing, install Node first (the Lhotse Face cards cover it).',
      mac: 'npm commands are identical on every system.',
      windows: 'Run npm inside WSL (Ubuntu) to match this course exactly. The npm commands themselves are the same everywhere.',
      chromeos: 'Run npm in the Linux environment; the commands are the same.',
      phone: 'Without a computer you can still check the package page on npmjs.com: exact name, weekly downloads, last publish, the **Dependencies** tab, and the repository link. `npm audit` needs a computer.',
    },
  },

  // ─────────────── Architecture, threats, secrets ───────────────
  'architecture-review': {
    term: 'Architecture review', short: 'Mapping the parts of a system and how data flows between them, then judging the weak spots.',
    what: "**Architecture** is the big picture of a system: which parts exist (an app, a server, a database, outside services) and how they talk to each other. An **architecture review** draws that picture and asks where it could break, leak or cost too much.\n\nBefore you judge any single part, you look at the whole system: where it could fail, and which parts everything else depends on.",
    how: "**1. Find the parts.** Clues are everywhere in a repo: the README's install steps, `package.json` dependencies (a web framework means a server), `.env.example` (every outside service needs a key), config files, folder names like `server/`, `client/`, `jobs/`.\n\n**2. Draw boxes and arrows.** A box per part, an arrow per connection. Label every arrow with **what data** travels, **which way**, and **whether it's encrypted** (https).\n\n**3. Ask the review questions:**\n• **Single points of failure:** which one box, if it goes down, takes everything with it?\n• **Where do secrets live?** Keys belong on the server, in environment variables. Anything sent to a browser or phone app is public: users can read it.\n• **Trust boundaries:** where does data cross from \"someone else's control\" into yours (a form, an upload, an API call)? Every crossing needs checking.\n• **Third parties:** which outside companies receive data, and which data?\n• **Backups and recovery:** if the database is lost, what then?\n• **Fit:** is it simpler or more complex than the job needs? Ten separate services for an app with 50 users is a cost and a risk, not a feature.\n• **Lock-in:** how hard would it be to move to another provider?",
    usage: [
      { code: '[Phone app] --https--> [API server] --> [Database]\n                            |\n                            +--> [Map tiles service]  (API key)\n                            +--> [Ad analytics]       (email + GPS!)', note: 'TrailPost on one screen. The last arrow is the finding: personal data leaves for an advertising company.' },
      { code: '// in the browser bundle\nconst MAPTILES_KEY = "mt_live_8f3k2q9zx"', note: 'A secret in front-end code is public: anyone can open devtools and read it. Server-side only.' },
      { code: 'DB_URL=\nMAPTILES_KEY=\nANALYTICS_ID=', note: 'A `.env.example` with empty values is a gift to an auditor: it lists every outside service the app talks to.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Understanding an unfamiliar project quickly before reading its code.', 'Spotting leaks: secrets in the front end, personal data going to third parties.', 'Planning your own app so it\'s simple, recoverable and cheap to run.'],
    when: ['At the start of any audit of a whole app or service, before the threat model.', 'Before you build something big, and again when you add a new outside service.'],
    whenNot: ['Don\'t spend hours on perfect diagrams. Boxes and arrows on paper are enough.', 'Don\'t judge architecture by fashion. "It doesn\'t use the newest framework" is not a finding; "the database has no backups" is.'],
    mistakes: [
      { prose: 'Putting an API key in front-end code because it "needs it to call the service".', why: 'Everything in a web page or app bundle can be read by users. Call the service from your server instead, and keep the key there.' },
      { prose: 'Reviewing only the code and ignoring the outside services.', why: 'Often the biggest risks are in the arrows: what you send to which company.' },
      { prose: 'Praising complexity as "scalable".', why: 'Every extra part is something to secure, pay for and fix at 3 a.m. Match the architecture to the real number of users.' },
    ],
    tips: ['List every outside domain the code talks to (search for `https://`). Each one is a box on your diagram.', 'Ask "what happens when this box is down?" for every box.'],
    related: ['threat-model', 'privacy-audit', 'cost-analysis', 'api'],
  },

  'threat-model': {
    term: 'Threat model', short: 'What are we protecting, from whom, and how could they get in? Then: what do we do about it?',
    what: "A **threat model** is a structured way of thinking like an attacker, so you can fix things before a real one does. It's planning, not paranoia: list what could go wrong, how likely it is, and what you will do about it.",
    how: "Answer four questions:\n\n**1. What are we protecting? (assets)** User accounts and passwords, personal data (emails, GPS), money, API keys, the database, the service staying online, your reputation.\n\n**2. Who might attack? (attackers)** Be realistic, not cinematic:\n• **bots** that scan every public site and repo automatically, all day (this is why \"nobody would target us\" is wrong)\n• **curious or malicious users** of the app itself\n• **a malicious package** in your dependencies\n• **an ex-teammate** whose access was never removed\n\n**3. How could they get in? (entry points)** Every place data or code enters: each route of the API, every form and upload, login, admin pages, install scripts, the CI system's tokens, the dependencies.\n\n**4. What could go wrong, and what will we do?** A handy checklist is **STRIDE**, one letter per kind of threat:\n• **S**poofing: pretending to be someone else (a stolen password)\n• **T**ampering: changing data you shouldn't (a delete route with no login)\n• **R**epudiation: doing something and denying it, because nothing was logged\n• **I**nformation disclosure: data leaking (a password in a public repo)\n• **D**enial of service: knocking the service offline (no limit on uploads)\n• **E**levation of privilege: getting powers you shouldn't (an admin page with no login)\n\nFor each threat, rate **impact** and **likelihood**, then write a **mitigation**: the fix or the defence.",
    usage: [
      { code: 'ASSET: users\' trip plans (where people will be, and when)\nATTACKER: anyone on the internet\nENTRY POINT: DELETE /trips/:id has no login check\nSTRIDE: Tampering\nIMPACT: high (trips vanish)   LIKELIHOOD: high (bots try every route)\nMITIGATION: require login, and check the trip belongs to the user', note: 'One threat, fully written up. Notice it ends with a fix.' },
      { code: 'S  Spoofing                stolen passwords          -> hashed passwords, 2-step login\nT  Tampering               unauthenticated DELETE    -> require login + ownership\nR  Repudiation             no record of deletions    -> log who did what\nI  Information disclosure  .env in the public repo   -> rotate keys, ignore .env\nD  Denial of service       unlimited upload size     -> size and rate limits\nE  Elevation of privilege  /admin with no login      -> admin-only check', note: 'STRIDE as a checklist for TrailPost. Each row: the threat, a real example, the mitigation.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Auditing an app: it tells you which findings matter most.', 'Designing your own app: decide where logins, limits and logs go before you write code.', 'Explaining risk to a non-technical person: "here\'s what we protect, here\'s from whom, here\'s our plan".'],
    when: ['Any time a system holds personal data, money or keys, or is reachable from the internet.', 'Before launch, and whenever you add a new entry point (a new route, upload or integration).'],
    whenNot: ['Don\'t invent movie-plot threats while ignoring boring ones. A leaked password in a repo is far likelier than a spy satellite.', 'Don\'t stop at a list of threats. A threat model without mitigations is just a worry list.'],
    mistakes: [
      { prose: '"We\'re too small for anyone to attack us."', why: 'Most attacks are automated. Bots scan every public address and repo without caring who owns it.' },
      { prose: 'Only thinking about outside hackers.', why: 'Malicious dependencies, leftover access for ex-teammates and simple mistakes cause many real incidents.' },
      { prose: 'Treating every threat as equally urgent.', why: 'Rate impact × likelihood, then fix the top ones first.' },
    ],
    tips: ['Start from the assets, not the attacks: "what would hurt most to lose?"', 'List every entry point. Routes that change data (POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE) with no login are the first thing to check.'],
    related: ['architecture-review', 'secret-scan', 'audit-method', 'http-methods-basics'],
  },

  'secret-scan': {
    term: 'Secret scan', short: 'Searching code, config and history for passwords and keys that should never be there.',
    what: "A **secret** is anything that grants access: passwords, API keys, tokens, database addresses with a password inside. A **secret scan** searches a project for them, because a secret in a repo is a secret anyone can copy.\n\nScanners work by **pattern matching**: they look for text that has the shape of a secret, like `password = \"...\"`. In JavaScript, patterns are written as **regular expressions** (regex).",
    how: "**Regex in four pieces** (enough for a scanner):\n• A regex is written between slashes: `/password/`. A trailing `i` makes it ignore case: `/password/i` matches `PASSWORD` and `Password`.\n• `|` means \"or\": `/password|secret|key|token/i`. Brackets group it: `(password|secret|key|token)`.\n• Special codes: `\\w` = a letter, digit or `_`; `\\s` = a space or tab; `*` = \"zero or more of the thing before\".\n• Square brackets mean \"any one of these characters\": `[\"']` = a double or a single quote.\n\nPut together: `/(password|secret|key|token)\\w*\\s*=\\s*[\"']/i` reads as: one of those words, maybe more name letters, maybe spaces, an `=`, maybe spaces, then a quote. That's \"a secret-looking name being given a text value\".\n\n`pattern.test(text)` returns `true` or `false`.\n\n**A scanner** reads each file, splits it into lines, tests each line and reports **file + line number**, but never prints the secret itself (or prints it masked: `mt_l****`).\n\n**History matters.** Git remembers every version. Deleting `.env` in a new commit leaves it readable in the old ones. `git log --all --full-history -- .env` shows every commit that ever touched it.\n\n**If a secret has leaked:** assume it has been copied (bots scrape public repos for keys very quickly). **Rotate it first**: revoke the old key and issue a new one. Cleaning the history comes second; it does nothing for copies already taken.\n\n**Real tools** (on real computers) do this with hundreds of patterns, including the exact shapes of popular services' keys: secret scanners such as gitleaks and trufflehog, and the secret scanning built into code hosts.",
    usage: [
      { code: 'const SECRET = /(password|secret|key|token)\\w*\\s*=\\s*["\']/i\nconsole.log(SECRET.test(\'const DB_PASSWORD = "hunter2"\'))\nconsole.log(SECRET.test("const tokens = 5"))\nconsole.log(SECRET.test("const port = 3000"))', out: 'true\nfalse\nfalse', note: 'Line 2 matches. `tokens = 5` has the word but no quote after the `=`. `port` has no secret word at all.' },
      { code: 'const lines = text.split("\\n")\nlines.forEach((line, i) => {\n  if (SECRET.test(line)) console.log("Possible secret on line " + (i + 1))\n})', out: 'Possible secret on line 3', note: 'The core of a scanner. Array indexes start at 0, line numbers at 1, hence `i + 1`.' },
      { code: '$ git log --all --full-history --oneline -- .env', out: '4c1d9e2 Remove .env\n9a7b3f0 Add config', note: 'Real computers. The file was "removed", but commit 9a7b3f0 still contains it. Anyone with the repo can read it.' },
      { code: '$ git log -p -S "hunter2" --oneline', note: 'Real computers. Finds every commit that added or removed that exact text, with the change shown (`-p`).' },
    ],
    uses: ['Every audit of a repository: check the files AND the history.', 'Before your own first push: scan, and make sure `.env` is in `.gitignore`.', 'In automatic checks that block a commit or push containing a secret.'],
    when: ['Before publishing any repo, and during every audit.', 'Immediately when you suspect a key was committed.'],
    whenNot: ['Don\'t paste the secrets you find into your report, a chat or an issue. Report the file and line; mask the value.', 'Don\'t treat a clean scan as proof. Patterns miss secrets with unusual names, and flag harmless lines (false positives). It\'s a net, not a guarantee.', 'Don\'t think deleting the file fixes a leak. The history and any copies remain: rotate the key.'],
    mistakes: [
      { prose: 'Deleting `.env` in a new commit and calling the leak fixed.', why: 'The old commit still holds it, and it may already be copied. Revoke and replace the key first.' },
      { prose: 'Trusting one simple pattern to find everything.', why: 'The pattern above misses JSON style (`"password": "x"` uses a colon) and keys stored under odd names. Real scanners use many patterns; humans still read the config files.' },
      { prose: 'Printing every found secret in full in the scan output.', why: 'Your scan output may be logged or shared. Report where, not what.' },
    ],
    tips: ['Check `.gitignore` early in any audit: if `.env` isn\'t listed, look for one.', 'A `.env` in a freshly cloned repo was committed: a clone only contains committed files.', 'Keep secrets in environment variables on the server, never in code.'],
    related: ['threat-model', 'gitignore', 'git-log', 'hidden-files'],
    os: {
      linux: 'The `git log` commands work as shown. Secret-scanning tools like gitleaks are packaged by many distributions: search with your package manager (`apt search gitleaks`, `dnf search gitleaks`, `pacman -Ss gitleaks`), or download a release binary from the project\'s releases page.',
      mac: 'The `git log` commands work as shown. With Homebrew: `brew install gitleaks`, then `gitleaks git` inside a repo scans its whole history (older versions call it `gitleaks detect`).',
      windows: 'Run git and scanners inside WSL (Ubuntu) and follow the Linux notes.',
      chromeos: 'In the Linux environment, follow the Linux notes (search with `apt search gitleaks`).',
      phone: 'On a phone you can still read `.gitignore` and browse a repo\'s files and commits in the browser. Running scanners needs a computer.',
    },
  },

  // ─────────────── Money ───────────────
  'cost-analysis': {
    term: 'Cost analysis', short: 'Working out what a tool or service will really cost: free tiers, per-use prices, hidden costs, and growth.',
    what: "**Cost analysis** turns a pricing page into a number for *your* usage, now and later. \"Free\" and \"$5 a month\" are headlines; the real cost depends on how much you use, what happens past the free limits, and the costs nobody puts on the pricing page.",
    how: "**1. List every billable unit.** Common ones: requests, compute time, storage (GB per month), **data out** (also called egress or bandwidth: data sent from the service to users), team seats, build minutes, emails sent, AI tokens.\n\n**2. Find the free allowance and price for each.** Pricing is usually \"the first N free each month, then $X per unit\".\n\n**3. Calculate per unit:** `cost = max(0, used − free) × price`. The `max(0, …)` matters: below the free limit, the cost is 0, not negative.\n\n**4. Add the units up**, then **project growth**: what if you have 10× the users? Free tiers make costs jump: you pay nothing, then suddenly pay for everything above the free limit.\n\n**5. Hunt the hidden costs:**\n• **data out** is often the surprise on the bill\n• **overages**: does it stop at the limit (a hard cap) or keep running and bill you?\n• free tiers that **pause** your project after inactivity, or delete data\n• **per-seat** pricing that grows with the team\n• paid-only features you will need: backups, custom domains, data export\n• **your time**: setup, maintenance, learning it\n• **exit cost (lock-in)**: how hard is it to leave? Can you export your data?\n\n**6. Protect yourself:** set a budget alert or spending cap before you launch. A bug that calls a paid API in a loop can run up a bill overnight.",
    usage: [
      { code: 'CloudCairn pricing (invented)        Your month       Cost\nrequests: 1M free, then $0.40/M       3.2M requests    (3.2 - 1) x 0.40 = $0.88\nstorage:  5 GB free, then $0.02/GB    12 GB            (12 - 5) x 0.02  = $0.14\ndata out: 100 GB free, then $0.09/GB  180 GB           (180 - 100) x 0.09 = $7.20\n                                                        total: $8.22', note: 'Every unit, its free allowance, your usage, the cost. Data out is 88% of the bill.' },
      { code: 'function costOver(used, free, price) {\n  return Math.max(0, used - free) * price\n}\nconsole.log(costOver(50, 100, 0.09))\nconsole.log(costOver(180, 100, 0.09).toFixed(2))', out: '0\n7.20', note: 'Under the free limit costs 0. `toFixed(2)` rounds to cents and returns a string.' },
      { code: 'At 10x traffic: requests 32M, data out 1800 GB\n(32 - 1) x 0.40 = $12.40\n(1800 - 100) x 0.09 = $153.00\nstorage still $0.14  ->  $165.54 per month', note: '10× the users, 20× the bill. Free tiers make growth expensive in jumps.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Choosing a hosting service, database or email provider.', 'Checking whether a "free forever" plan fits your real usage.', 'Planning a launch: knowing your bill at 100, 1,000 and 10,000 users.', 'Spotting the one unit that dominates the bill, so you know what to optimise.'],
    when: ['Before committing to any paid or "free tier" service.', 'Before a launch or marketing push that could multiply traffic.'],
    whenNot: ['Don\'t compare only headline prices. Two "$5" plans can differ by 10× once your real usage is plugged in.', 'Don\'t optimise pennies while ignoring hours. A service that costs $3 more but saves you a day a month is cheaper.', 'Don\'t launch a paid service with no budget alert or cap.'],
    mistakes: [
      { prose: 'Forgetting data out (egress).', why: 'Storage is cheap; sending it to users often isn\'t. Images and video make data out the biggest line.' },
      { prose: 'Believing "unlimited".', why: 'Read the terms: "unlimited" plans usually have a fair-use limit, after which they slow you down or bill you.' },
      { wrong: 'cost = (used - free) * price   // used is below free', result: '-0.45', why: 'Below the free allowance the cost is 0, not negative. Use `Math.max(0, used - free) * price`.' },
    ],
    tips: ['Use real numbers from your logs or analytics, not guesses.', 'Price the 10× scenario before launch. If the number scares you, plan now.', 'Check the exit: can you export everything on the free plan?'],
    related: ['architecture-review', 'claims-evidence', 'audit-report'],
  },

  // ─────────────── Data and law ───────────────
  'privacy-audit': {
    term: 'Privacy audit', short: 'Finding what personal data a project collects, where it goes, who sees it, and how long it\'s kept.',
    what: "A **privacy audit** follows personal data through a system: what's collected, why, where it's stored, which other companies receive it, how long it's kept, and whether people can see or delete it. Then it compares that with what the project **promises** in its privacy policy.\n\n**Personal data** is anything that identifies a person or can be linked to one: name, email, phone number, IP address, precise location, photos, and also \"anonymous\" data that becomes identifying when combined (a GPS track that starts and ends at the same house every day).",
    how: "Build a **data inventory**, one row per kind of data:\n• **What:** the field (email, GPS location, trip notes)\n• **Why:** the purpose. If there's no good reason to collect it, that's a finding (**data minimisation**: collect only what you need)\n• **Where stored:** which database, logs, backups\n• **Who receives it:** every third party (analytics, error tracking, email sending, ads)\n• **How long:** the retention period. \"Logs kept 10 years\" is a finding.\n• **Who can access it** inside the team, and can users **export or delete** it?\n\n**Where to look for evidence:**\n• the privacy policy (the claims)\n• the code: every `fetch` to another domain, every analytics or tracking package in package.json\n• `.env` / `.env.example`: each outside service is a possible recipient\n• logs and database tables: what's actually saved\n• devtools' **Network** tab on the live app: every request it makes, and to whom\n\n**Extra-sensitive data** needs extra care: precise location, health, anything about children, passwords, ID documents.\n\nMany places have privacy laws (for example, the EU's GDPR) that give people rights to see and delete their data and limit what companies may do with it. This card is general education, not legal advice.",
    usage: [
      { code: 'DATA        WHY            STORED        SENT TO                    KEPT\nemail       login          database      collect.adwhale.example    forever\nGPS         show on map    database      collect.adwhale.example    forever\nIP address  (none given)   server logs   nobody                     10 years', note: 'TrailPost\'s inventory. Two findings jump out: data sent to an ad company, and a 10-year log retention with no stated purpose.' },
      { code: 'function track(user, event) {\n  fetch("https://collect.adwhale.example/v2/event", {\n    method: "POST",\n    body: JSON.stringify({ email: user.email, gps: user.lastLocation, event })\n  })\n}', note: 'The evidence: the code sends email and GPS to an outside company on every event. The policy says "we collect nothing". Contradicted.' },
      { code: '"We collect nothing. Your trips stay on your device."', note: 'A claim to test. An app with a server and a database is already storing trips somewhere other than your device.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Auditing any app that has user accounts or tracks behaviour.', 'Checking a tool before you hand it your users\' data (an email service, analytics, a chat widget).', 'Writing an honest privacy policy for your own app.'],
    when: ['Whenever personal data is involved, which is nearly every app with a login.', 'Before adding any analytics, tracking or outside service to your own project.'],
    whenNot: ['Don\'t trust the privacy policy alone. Policies are claims; the code and network traffic are evidence.', 'Don\'t collect data "in case it\'s useful later". Data you don\'t have can\'t leak.', 'Don\'t give legal conclusions ("this breaks the law"). Report what the data flow is; legal judgement needs a lawyer.'],
    mistakes: [
      { prose: 'Forgetting logs and backups.', why: 'Server logs often hold IP addresses, emails or even passwords from bad requests, and backups keep deleted data alive.' },
      { prose: 'Calling data "anonymous" because the name was removed.', why: 'Precise locations, timestamps and device details can identify a person on their own. Location tracks reveal homes and workplaces.' },
      { prose: 'Adding an analytics package without checking what it sends.', why: 'Some send every page view with identifying details to their own servers. You become responsible for that flow.' },
    ],
    tips: ['The safest data is data you never collect.', 'Search the code for `https://` to list every outside domain that might receive data.', 'Compare the policy line by line with your inventory. Every mismatch is a finding.'],
    related: ['architecture-review', 'claims-evidence', 'license-check', 'devtools'],
  },

  'license-check': {
    term: 'Licence check', short: 'Checking that the licences of a project and its dependencies allow what you plan to do with them.',
    what: "A **licence** (spelled *license* in code and US English) is the permission slip that comes with someone else's code. It says what you may do: use it, change it, sell products with it, and what you must do in return.\n\nThe key surprise: **no licence means no permission.** Code on a public website with no licence is still \"all rights reserved\" by its author. You may read it, but you may not legally copy it into your project.\n\nThis card is general education, not legal advice. For anything commercial or high-stakes, ask a lawyer.",
    how: "**The main families:**\n• **Permissive** (MIT, ISC, BSD, Apache-2.0): use it for almost anything, including closed-source and paid products. Main duty: keep the copyright and licence notice. **Apache-2.0** adds an explicit patent grant, and asks you to keep its NOTICE file and mark files you changed. ISC is npm's default for new packages and works like MIT.\n• **Weak copyleft** (LGPL, MPL-2.0): you can use the library in a closed product, but changes you make to the library's own files must be shared under the same licence.\n• **Strong copyleft** (GPL-2.0, GPL-3.0): if you **distribute** software that includes GPL code (ship an app, sell a download), the whole combined program must be released under the GPL, with source code. Running it privately, or on your own server, is not distributing.\n• **Network copyleft** (AGPL-3.0): closes that server gap. If users interact with a **modified** AGPL program over a network (say, your web app), you must offer them its source code.\n• **Source-available** (such as the Business Source License, the Server Side Public License, or custom \"free for non-commercial use\" terms): you can read the code, but use is restricted, often \"no competing hosted service\" or \"no commercial use\". These are not open source by the standard definition, even though the code is public.\n\n**In package.json**, the `license` field uses short standard names called **SPDX identifiers**: `\"MIT\"`, `\"Apache-2.0\"`, `\"GPL-3.0-only\"`, `\"AGPL-3.0-only\"`. Two traps:\n• `\"UNLICENSED\"` means **proprietary: no permission to use it**\n• `\"Unlicense\"` (no D) is a different thing: a public-domain-style licence\n\n**The check:** read the project's LICENSE file and package.json, then **every dependency's** licence (transitive ones too), and compare with your plan: closed-source app? paid product? web service? Licences also cover fonts, images, datasets and AI models.",
    usage: [
      { code: '$ npm view express license', out: 'MIT', note: 'Real computers. Ask the registry for one package\'s licence without installing it.' },
      { code: '$ cat node_modules/trail-maps/package.json', out: '{\n  "name": "trail-maps",\n  "version": "3.0.1",\n  "license": "AGPL-3.0-only"\n}', note: 'Works in the practice terminal. A network-copyleft dependency inside a web app: if TrailPost modifies it, it must offer users that source.' },
      { code: '$ npx license-checker --summary', out: '├─ MIT: 61\n├─ ISC: 9\n├─ Apache-2.0: 3\n├─ AGPL-3.0-only: 1\n└─ UNLICENSED: 1', note: 'Real computers. Counts the licences across the whole installed tree. (npx downloads and runs a package, so it\'s a trust decision too.)' },
      { code: '"license": "UNLICENSED"', note: 'Proprietary. Unless you have a separate agreement, you have no right to use this package.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Before adding a dependency to a product you will sell or keep closed-source.', 'Before copying code from a repo, gist or forum into your project.', 'Auditing a project\'s whole dependency tree for licences that clash with its plans.', 'Choosing a licence for your own project (MIT is a common, simple choice).'],
    when: ['Every time code, fonts, images or models from someone else enter your project.', 'Before going commercial: what was fine for a hobby may not be fine for a product.'],
    whenNot: ['Don\'t assume "it\'s on a public repo, so it\'s free to use". No licence = no permission.', 'Don\'t treat this card as legal advice for a real business decision. Use it to spot questions, then ask someone qualified.', 'Don\'t only check your direct dependencies. A licence problem deep in the tree is still in your product.'],
    mistakes: [
      { prose: 'Thinking the GPL forbids selling software.', why: 'It doesn\'t. You may sell GPL software, but when you distribute it, you must also provide the source under the GPL.' },
      { prose: 'Reading `"UNLICENSED"` as "no licence restrictions".', why: 'It means the opposite: proprietary, all rights reserved. `"Unlicense"` (no D) is the public-domain-style one.' },
      { prose: 'Calling a source-available project "open source" in your report.', why: 'Its terms may forbid exactly what you plan, like running it as a hosted service. Read the actual licence text.' },
    ],
    tips: ['Check the licence before you fall in love with a library, not after you\'ve built on it.', 'Keep a list of licences your project accepts (for example MIT, ISC, BSD, Apache-2.0) and flag anything else for review.', 'When you publish your own code, add a LICENSE file. Without one, nobody may legally use it.'],
    related: ['dependency-audit', 'privacy-audit', 'package-json', 'github'],
    os: {
      linux: '`npm view` and `npx license-checker` work the same on every system. To read a LICENSE file in the terminal: `cat LICENSE` or `less LICENSE`.',
      mac: '`npm view` and `npx license-checker` work the same on every system.',
      windows: 'Run npm commands inside WSL (Ubuntu); they behave the same.',
      chromeos: 'Run npm commands in the Linux environment; they behave the same.',
      phone: 'Code hosts show the detected licence in the repo\'s sidebar, and npmjs.com shows **License** on every package page. Both are readable on a phone.',
    },
  },

  // ─────────────── Numbers and the write-up ───────────────
  'benchmark-skepticism': {
    term: 'Benchmark skepticism', short: 'Treating speed and performance numbers as claims: what was measured, how, by whom, and is it typical?',
    what: "A **benchmark** is a measurement of performance: how fast, how many requests per second, how much memory. Benchmarks are useful, and also the easiest numbers to bend. **Benchmark skepticism** means asking how the number was made before you believe it, especially when the person who made it is selling the faster thing.",
    how: "Ask these questions of every benchmark:\n• **What exactly was measured?** \"Faster\" at what: start-up, one request, a thousand at once?\n• **Who ran it?** A vendor's benchmark of its own product is a claim, not independent evidence.\n• **Same conditions?** Same hardware, versions, settings and data for both sides?\n• **Realistic workload?** A tiny test record vs real trips with photos.\n• **How many runs, and which one is shown?** One run proves little. Quoting only the **best** run is **cherry-picking**.\n• **Warm or cold?** A second request is often served from a cache (a saved copy) and looks impossibly fast. Real users often hit the cold path.\n• **Can you reproduce it?** Is the benchmark script published?\n\n**Better numbers to ask for:**\n• **median**: sort the results and take the middle one. Unlike the average (mean), one freak result can't drag it.\n• **p95**: 95% of requests were faster than this. It shows what your slowest regular users feel.\n\n**Watch the chart:** a y-axis that doesn't start at zero can make a 3% difference look like a cliff.\n\n**Best of all: measure it yourself**, with your own data, several runs, and report the median.",
    usage: [
      { code: '| run | trailpost | routebook |\n|  1  |    410    |    220    |\n|  2  |    395    |    240    |\n|  3  |     22    |    230    |\n|  4  |    405    |    235    |\n|  5  |    400    |    225    |', note: 'Load times in ms (lower is better). The claim "10× faster: 22 ms vs 220 ms" uses TrailPost\'s single best run (probably a cached request). Medians: 400 vs 230, so RouteBook is about 1.7× faster.' },
      { code: 'function median(nums) {\n  const s = [...nums].sort((a, b) => a - b)\n  const mid = Math.floor(s.length / 2)\n  return s.length % 2 ? s[mid] : (s[mid - 1] + s[mid]) / 2\n}\nconsole.log(median([410, 395, 22, 405, 400]))\nconsole.log(median([5, 1, 9, 7]))', out: '400\n6', note: 'Sort numerically (copying first so the original isn\'t changed), then take the middle; with an even count, average the two middle values.' },
      { code: 'console.time("load")\nloadTrips()\nconsole.timeEnd("load")', out: 'load: 41.732ms', note: 'Measure it yourself. Run it several times and take the median. (Your number will differ.)' },
    ],
    uses: ['Judging "X is 10× faster than Y" claims before switching tools.', 'Checking your own performance improvements honestly.', 'Reading launch posts, landing pages and comparison charts.'],
    when: ['Whenever a decision depends on a performance number.', 'Whenever the benchmark was made by someone with something to sell.'],
    whenNot: ['Don\'t dismiss every benchmark. A published, reproducible script with medians and many runs is good evidence.', 'Don\'t switch tools for a speed gain your users would never notice. 20 ms vs 30 ms rarely matters; 4 s vs 400 ms does.'],
    mistakes: [
      { prose: 'Comparing averages when one run is a freak outlier.', why: 'One 22 ms cached run drags an average down a lot. The median ignores it.' },
      { wrong: '[10, 9, 100].sort()', result: '[ 10, 100, 9 ]', why: 'JavaScript\'s default sort compares as text. For numbers, use `.sort((a, b) => a - b)`.' },
      { prose: 'Trusting a dramatic chart without reading its axis.', why: 'An axis starting at 95 instead of 0 makes tiny differences look huge.' },
    ],
    tips: ['Always ask for the median (and p95), never only the best run.', 'Repeat your own measurement at least five times. Throw away the first run if it warms a cache.', 'Write down the hardware and versions next to every number you report.'],
    related: ['claims-evidence', 'audit-report', 'array-methods'],
  },

  'audit-report': {
    term: 'Audit report', short: 'The write-up: verdict first, then each finding with severity, evidence and a fix.',
    what: "The **audit report** is what's left when the audit is over. It's how your work turns into a decision. A good report lets a busy reader get the answer in ten seconds, and lets a careful reader re-check every finding.",
    how: "**The structure:**\n1. **Title, date and version audited** (\"TrailPost 2.3.0, commit 9a7b3f0, audited 1 June 2026\"). Software changes; say exactly what you looked at.\n2. **Summary**: the verdict and the top findings in plain English, in three or four sentences. **Bottom line up front**: many readers stop here.\n3. **Scope and method**: what you checked, how, and what you did **not** check.\n4. **Findings**, most severe first. Each one has:\n   • an **ID and title** (\"K-01: Database password committed in .env\")\n   • a **severity**: CRITICAL, HIGH, MEDIUM, LOW, or INFO\n   • **evidence**: file and line, command and output, exact quote\n   • **impact**: what could happen, in plain words\n   • **fix**: a specific recommendation\n5. **What's good**: honest positives (a clear licence, tests, quick issue replies). It shows you were fair.\n6. **Verdict and conditions**: ADOPT / ADOPT WITH FIXES / DO NOT ADOPT, and what would change it.\n\n**Tone:** factual, specific, no blame, no insults. Write \"observed\" and \"unverified\", not \"they lied\". Never include a working secret in the report: mask it.\n\n**Security problems in someone else's project:** report them **privately** to the maintainers first (look for `SECURITY.md`), and give them time to fix before anything is public. This is called **responsible disclosure**.",
    usage: [
      { code: '## K-01  Database password committed in .env      HIGH\nEvidence: .env is present in a fresh clone (line 1: DB_URL=postgres://admin:****@...).\n          .gitignore lists only node_modules.\nImpact:   anyone with the repo can read and delete every user\'s trips.\nFix:      rotate the database password now; add .env to .gitignore;\n          move secrets to environment variables on the server.', note: 'One finding, complete: title, severity, evidence (with the password masked), impact, fix.' },
      { code: 'SUMMARY\nVerdict: DO NOT ADOPT. TrailPost leaks its database password, its install\nscript uploads your .env to an outside server, and its privacy claim is\ncontradicted by code that sends email and GPS to an ad company.\nRe-audit after K-01 to K-03 are fixed.', note: 'Bottom line up front: the verdict, the reasons, and what would change it.' },
      { code: 'CRITICAL  exploitable now, severe harm (e.g. live admin password public)\nHIGH      serious harm, realistic to exploit\nMEDIUM    real risk under some conditions\nLOW       minor, or hard to exploit\nINFO      not a risk; worth knowing', note: 'A severity scale. Put it in the report so readers know what your words mean.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Sharing an audit with a team, a client or your future self.', 'Opening a clear, fixable issue on a project (one finding per issue).', 'The final step of your Summit project: auditing your own app and writing it up.'],
    when: ['At the end of every audit, even a 20-minute one (then it\'s five lines).', 'Whenever a decision needs to be justified to someone else.'],
    whenNot: ['Don\'t post security findings publicly before the maintainers have had a chance to fix them.', 'Don\'t pad the report. Ten precise findings beat fifty vague ones.', 'Don\'t write a verdict you can\'t back with the findings above it.'],
    mistakes: [
      { prose: 'Burying the verdict on page five.', why: 'Most readers read only the top. Put the verdict and the reason first.' },
      { prose: 'Findings with no fix.', why: 'A problem without a recommendation leaves the reader stuck. Say what to do.' },
      { prose: 'Pasting the actual leaked password as "evidence".', why: 'You\'ve just leaked it again. Show enough to prove it (the file, line, first characters) and mask the rest.' },
    ],
    tips: ['Write the summary last, but put it first.', 'One finding = one problem. If you need "and also", split it.', 'Re-read every finding asking: could someone else find this again from my evidence alone?'],
    related: ['audit-method', 'claims-evidence', 'threat-model'],
  },

  // ─────────────── Working with an AI coding assistant ───────────────
  'ai-coding-assistant': {
    term: 'AI coding assistant', short: 'A tool that writes or changes code from your description. It is fast, and you are still the one who checks and ships the result.',
    what: "An **AI coding assistant** (or **coding agent**) reads the files in your project and writes or edits code when you describe what you want. It predicts likely code from your words and the files it can see. It does not know what you meant beyond what you told it, and it does not understand your project the way you do.\n\nThat makes it fast and useful, and it makes its mistakes look just as polished as its good work. You are the reviewer. Treat what it gives you like a pull request from a stranger: read it, run it, and only then accept it.",
    how: "**Write a brief.** Five parts:\n1. **Goal:** one sentence saying what should be true afterwards.\n2. **Constraints:** what it must not do (no new packages, keep the existing function names, no changes outside `src/`).\n3. **Files:** which files to read and change.\n4. **What done looks like:** concrete inputs and outputs, such as `slug('Hello World')` returns `'hello-world'`.\n5. **How to test:** the command you will run, such as `npm test`.\n\n**Ask for small changes.** One fix or one feature at a time, small enough that you can read the whole diff in a few minutes. A huge change can't really be reviewed.\n\n**Protect yourself.** Commit before you start (`git commit -am \"Checkpoint\"`), so `git restore .` can undo uncommitted edits to tracked files. Keep secrets out of prompts: what you paste is sent to a service (unless the tool runs on your own computer). Paste config with placeholders instead of real values.\n\n**Know when to stop.** Stop when you have asked three times and it keeps going in circles, when the fix is smaller than the prompt, when the code touches logins, payments or encryption and you can't review it, or when you can't explain what it wrote. Do it yourself, or learn the part first.",
    usage: [
      { code: "Goal: add slug(name) in src/slug.js.\nConstraints: no new packages; don't change other files.\nDone when: slug('Hello World') returns 'hello-world'\n  and slug('  Mt  Everest ') returns 'mt-everest'.\nTest: I will run npm test.", note: 'A short brief. Every line closes off one way it could go wrong.' },
      { code: '$ git commit -am "Checkpoint before the assistant works"\n# ...the assistant makes changes you dislike...\n$ git restore .', note: 'Commit first. If the result is a mess, one command puts your tracked files back. (New files it created stay: check `git status`.)' },
      { code: 'My config, with real values removed:\nDATABASE_URL=<redacted>\nAPI_KEY=<redacted>\nPORT=3000\nThe server crashes at start with "API_KEY is not set". Why?', note: 'Enough to get help. The real secrets never leave your computer.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Writing a first version of a small function or a test.', 'Explaining unfamiliar code, error messages or a library.', 'Doing repetitive edits across many files, with you reviewing the diff.'],
    when: ['The task is small and you can describe a clear pass or fail.', 'You will read and run everything it produces.', 'You have committed first.'],
    whenNot: ["Don't hand it code you can't review, such as login, payments or encryption, and ship the result unread.", "Don't paste secrets, customer data or private keys.", "Don't ask for a whole app in one go.", "Don't keep going when it has failed three times on the same thing."],
    mistakes: [
      { wrong: 'Add login, payments, an admin dashboard and email alerts to my app.', result: '(a change across dozens of files that nobody can review)', why: 'Split it into steps, each one small enough to read and test on its own.' },
      { prose: 'Pasting a whole `.env` file into the chat.', why: 'Your real keys are now on another company\'s servers. Revoke them and make new ones, and paste placeholders next time.' },
      { prose: 'Accepting a change because the assistant said "this should work".', why: '"Should" is a guess. Run it.' },
    ],
    tips: ['Put the brief in a file such as `TASK.md`, so you can reuse and improve it.', 'If the result is wrong, change the brief, not just the wording of your complaint. Say what was missing.', 'Keep your tests. They are how you check work you did not write.'],
    related: ['reviewing-ai-code', 'package-hallucination', 'git-restore', 'secrets-management'],
  },

  'reviewing-ai-code': {
    term: 'Reviewing AI-written code', short: 'Read the whole diff, run the tests and the app yourself, and try the edges. "Done" and "all tests pass" are claims, not evidence.',
    what: "**Reviewing** code you did not write is the same job whether a person or an assistant wrote it. With an assistant, three things get more likely: it writes plausible code that is subtly wrong, it changes more than you asked, and it tells you it worked when it didn't check.\n\nSo you verify. You never need to trust its summary, because the diff and your own test run show what really happened.",
    how: "**1. Read the whole diff.** Start with `git diff --stat` to see which files changed. Any file you did not ask about needs an explanation. Then read every changed line, **deleted lines too**. Look for a new dependency in `package.json`, a removed or weakened test, and anything that looks like a key or password.\n\n**2. Run it yourself.** Run `npm test`, then use the app by hand. Compare the test count with before: a run that says all pass, but with fewer tests, means tests were removed.\n\n**3. Try the edges.** Empty input, a missing field, a negative number, a very long string, the wrong type. The assistant often tests only the example you gave it.\n\n**Common failures to look for:**\n• **Invented functions and packages:** a method that does not exist (`TypeError: items.sortBy is not a function`) or a package nobody published.\n• **Outdated APIs:** code in an old style, or using a feature a newer version removed or deprecated. Check the docs for the version you actually use.\n• **Passes the example, fails the rest:** `average([2, 4])` works, `average([])` throws.\n• **Deleted or weakened tests:** removed `test(...)` blocks, `test.skip`, or an exact check replaced by `toBeTruthy()`.\n• **Hard-coded secrets:** a key or password written in the source.\n• **Swallowed errors:** an empty `catch {}` that hides a failure.",
    usage: [
      { code: ' package.json        | 1 +\n src/server.js       | 6 +++++-\n test/server.test.js | 6 ------\n 3 files changed, 6 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)', note: '`git diff --stat`. A test file that only loses lines is the first thing to open.' },
      { code: "-  expect(total([1, 2, 3])).toBe(6)\n+  expect(total([1, 2, 3])).toBeTruthy()", note: 'A weakened test: it used to demand 6. Now it accepts anything that is not zero.' },
      { code: '$ npm test        # before\nTests:       12 passed, 12 total\n$ npm test        # after the assistant\nTests:       9 passed, 9 total', note: 'Everything passes, yet three tests are gone. Compare the counts. (This is Jest-style output; other test tools print it differently.)' },
      { code: "function average(nums) {\n  return nums.reduce((a, b) => a + b) / nums.length\n}\nconsole.log(average([2, 4]))\nconsole.log(average([]))", out: "3\nTypeError: Reduce of empty array with no initial value", note: 'The given example passes. The empty list, which nobody asked about, crashes it.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Checking an assistant\'s change before you commit it.', 'Reviewing a teammate\'s pull request, using the same checklist.', 'Deciding whether a generated test really tests anything.'],
    when: ['Every time. Review before you commit, and again before you ship.'],
    whenNot: ["Don't review only the added lines. Deleted lines hide the worst surprises.", "Don't treat a passing test run as proof if you haven't looked at what the tests check."],
    mistakes: [
      { prose: 'Skimming the summary the assistant wrote instead of reading the diff.', why: 'The summary says what it meant to do. The diff shows what it did.' },
      { prose: 'Only running the example from your prompt.', why: 'It was written to pass that example. Try inputs it was not told about.' },
      { prose: 'Accepting a change that touches files you never mentioned.', why: 'Ask why. Extra changes are where deleted tests and new dependencies hide.' },
    ],
    tips: ['`git diff -- path/to/file` shows one file at a time. Read the small diffs first.', 'Write the test yourself first, then ask the assistant to make it pass. It cannot weaken a test you check afterwards.', 'If a diff is too big to read, it is too big to accept. Ask for smaller steps.'],
    related: ['ai-coding-assistant', 'package-hallucination', 'git-diff', 'secret-scan', 'edge-cases'],
  },

  'package-hallucination': {
    term: 'Package hallucination', short: 'An assistant names a package that does not exist. Installing an invented name is a security risk, because an attacker can publish a package under it.',
    what: "Assistants sometimes write `require('slugify-pro')` or suggest `npm install fast-trail-utils` for a package that was never published. Others name a real package that does something different from what the code assumes.\n\nThe invented name is a security problem as well as a bug. Anyone can publish a package on npm under any name that is free. An attacker who notices that assistants keep suggesting a certain made-up name can publish a package with that name. The next person to run `npm install` on it gets the attacker's code, and install scripts run with your user's permissions. Typo-lookalike names (`expresss`) are the same trick.",
    how: "Before you install any package an assistant suggested:\n1. **Is it already in `package.json`?** If the code requires something that isn't listed and isn't a built-in module, treat it as suspect.\n2. **Does it exist?** `npm view <name>` prints its details, or an E404 error if there is no such package.\n3. **Is it what it should be?** Check how old it is (`npm view <name> time.created`), who maintains it (`npm view <name> maintainers`), its weekly downloads and its repository link on the npm page. A package created last week, with a handful of downloads and no repository, is a warning sign.\n4. **Do you need it at all?** Prefer a well-known package, a built-in module, or three lines of your own code.\n5. **Install carefully:** `npm install --ignore-scripts <name>` stops install scripts from running while you look at what you got.\n\nThe diff check from this trail does step 1 automatically: it flags a `require` of a package that is not in `package.json`.",
    usage: [
      { code: "+const slugify = require('slugify-pro')", note: 'A diff line. If `slugify-pro` is not in package.json, stop and look it up.' },
      { code: "$ npm view slugify-pro\nnpm error code E404\nnpm error 404 Not Found - GET https://registry.npmjs.org/slugify-pro - Not found", note: 'There is no such package: the assistant invented it. (The exact lines vary a little by npm version.)' },
      { code: "$ npm view trail-slug time.created\n2026-10-02T09:41:17.512Z\n$ npm view trail-slug maintainers\n[ 'anon-4410 <anon4410@mail.example>' ]", note: 'It exists, but it was published a few weeks ago by one unknown person. That deserves a closer look before it goes near your project. (The package and maintainer here are invented.)' },
      { code: '$ npm install --ignore-scripts trail-slug', note: 'Installs the package but does not run its install scripts, so you can inspect it first.' },
    ],
    uses: ['Checking every dependency an assistant adds.', 'Spotting a typo-lookalike name before it gets installed.', 'Deciding whether to use a package or to write the few lines yourself.'],
    when: ['Any time new code needs a package that is not in package.json yet.'],
    whenNot: ["Don't run `npm install` on a name you have not looked up.", "Don't treat an old, popular, well-known package as suspicious just because the assistant suggested it. Check the unfamiliar ones."],
    mistakes: [
      { wrong: 'npm install slugify-pro', result: 'npm error 404 Not Found - GET https://registry.npmjs.org/slugify-pro - Not found', why: 'The name does not exist, so the assistant invented it. You were lucky: if someone had published it, npm would have installed their code.' },
      { wrong: "const sortBy = require('array-sort-by-utils')   (never published)", result: "Error: Cannot find module 'array-sort-by-utils'", why: 'Running the code shows an unpublished name at once. The dangerous case is a name that does exist and does something else.' },
      { prose: 'Installing a package because it has a convincing name and a short README.', why: 'Both are easy to fake. Age, maintainers, downloads and the repository are harder to fake.' },
    ],
    tips: ['A package with no repository link, no history and a few downloads is not necessarily bad. It just has not earned trust yet.', 'Pin the version of a package you have checked, and commit the lock file so everybody installs the same code.'],
    related: ['ai-coding-assistant', 'reviewing-ai-code', 'dependency-audit', 'package-json'],
  },
}
